 Performance optimization:
 Strategies Employed to Optimize Database Performance
 To ensure efficient handling of player scores and competition results, several 
performance optimization strategies were implemented. The database schema was 
normalized to Third Normal Form (3NF) to eliminate redundancy while maintaining 
data integrity. Query optimization techniques, such as minimizing joins and 
selecting only required attributes, were used to reduce query execution time. 
Additionally, caching mechanisms can be employed in future development to 
improve retrieval speeds for frequently accessed leaderboard data.
 Indexing Techniques
 Indexing plays a vital role in improving database performance, especially for 
leaderboard queries where frequent sorting and ranking operations are performed. 
The following indexing techniques were applied:
  Primary Indexes: Defined on unique keys such as Player_ID and 
Competition_ID to enable quick lookups.
  Composite Indexes: Implemented on columns commonly used together in 
queries, such as (Competition_ID, Score) to speed up ranking queries.
  Clustered Indexing: Considered on the Score attribute to enhance the 
efficiency of ordering operations when generating leaderboards.
 Evaluation Under Load Conditions
 The chosen normalization level (3NF) provides a balance between data consistency 
and performance. Under light to moderate loads (few hundred players and 
competitions), the database demonstrates efficient query execution with minimal 
latency. However, under heavy loads (tens of thousands of players and scores), query 
response times may slow due to frequent joins and ranking operations. In such cases, 
denormalization of certain tables or the use of materialized views may be necessary 
to optimize read-heavy operations.
 Overall, the database design achieves satisfactory performance for the current 
system scope, while leaving room for future enhancements such as sharding, 
partitioning, or distributed databases to support large-scale gaming environments.
